{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_qpxf7qsujit2nv3gnfbhdffa3umai24k4cd5264n6nypyghllila","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The output exactly matches what the C version produces. Let me summarize what was implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `src/coroutine.rs`, replacing every `unimplemented!()` with a working pure-Rust port of the C coroutine library.\n\n**Implementation strategy:** The original C uses `ucontext_t` for stack switching, which has no safe Rust equivalent. To stay in safe Rust without `extern \"C\"` calls, each coroutine runs on its own OS thread, and control is handed back-and-forth between the scheduler and the coroutine using a pair of mpsc channels:\n\n- `resume_tx` / `resume_rx`: scheduler signals a coroutine to run\n- `yield_tx` / `yield_rx`: coroutine signals \"yielded\" (still alive) or \"finished\"\n\nChannels are tracked in a global `Mutex<HashMap<(schedule_addr, id), CoroChannels>>`. When a coroutine is running, the scheduler is parked on `recv()`, and vice-versa, so access to `Schedule` is always strictly sequential, mirroring the cooperative semantics of the C version.\n\n**Key details:**\n- `coroutine_open` / `coroutine_close`: allocate/free the schedule, with `close` purging registry entries (dropping `resume_tx` causes any still-suspended coroutine threads to exit cleanly).\n- `coroutine_new`: matches the C slot-allocation logic, including the doubling-and-resize path when the table is full.\n- `coroutine_resume`: handles both `READY` (spawn the thread) and `SUSPEND` (re-send to the parked thread); on `Finished`, mirrors the C cleanup (`co[id]=None; --nco; running=-1`).\n- `coroutine_yield`: uses a `thread_local!` `TlsData` so the coroutine thread can find its own channels without any unsafe statics.\n- One small `unsafe` block converts the `Schedule` raw-pointer plus the `dyn Any` data pointer back to references on the coroutine thread — necessary because `dyn Any` isn't `Send`. The `unsafe` is sound because the scheduler is parked while the coroutine runs and the data lives in a heap `Box` owned by the `Coroutine` (which outlives the thread).\n\n`cargo build` and `timeout 60 cargo test` both pass; the `test_main` test produces the same `coroutine 0 : 0 / coroutine 1 : 100 / …` interleaving as the original C program."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":401,"cache_read_input_tokens":29323,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":401,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13194352-bbc2-418c-8eba-e879ee185289","uuid":"1abcb6ae-22f2-413b-a4e0-2515bb3b2628"}
